<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upanishads</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Upanishads are philosophical texts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> that form the latest layer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the oldest of them composed from around 700 BCE in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Through dialogues between teachers and pupils they explore the identity of the self, atman, with the ultimate reality, brahman, and develop ideas of rebirth and liberation. They became the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and of much later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upanishads.jpg" alt="Upanishads" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_and_DiscipleI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
